850090434c340e1c230f4546c0f428cbd0c5a73059242","file":"preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Cell-Bounded-Contingency-Tables-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","line":21,"producer_hash_method":"atlas-whitespace-normalized-text","producer_statement_sha256":"efffbac17fc1581bdecec171724910f43c9d51d6d57184ea380a8730be046788","provenance":{"kind":"computed","pin":"fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb","source":"preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Cell-Bounded-Contingency-Tables-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex"},"sha256":"sha256:bb08f9a3db7807be518850090434c340e1c230f4546c0f428cbd0c5a73059242","url":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Cell-Bounded-Contingency-Tables-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex#L21"},"statement_kind":"theorem","text":"\\label{thm:main}\nThere is one randomized algorithm with the following guarantee. Given\n$(r,c,b)$ as above and rational $\\varepsilon,\\delta\\in(0,1)$, it outputs\na nonnegative rational $\\widehat Z$ such that\n\\[\n \\P\\bigl((1-\\varepsilon)Z(r,c,b)\\le \\widehat Z\n                  \\le(1+\\varepsilon)Z(r,c,b)\\bigr)\\ge1-\\delta.\n\\]\nIf $\\Omega(r,c,b)$ is empty, the output is zero on every execution.\nThe algorithm uses independent unbiased bits, and one fixed polynomial\nin $L$, $\\varepsilon^{-1}$, and $\\log(\\delta^{-1})$ bounds its total bit\noperations on every execution, where $L$ is the total binary input\nlength, including the accuracy parameters. No counting or sampling\noracle is required."}
